<DocsLayout
  title="Scenes & layers"
  description="Organize your composition into layered scenes with displays and effects."
>
  <h2>What a scene is</h2>
  <p>
    A scene is a container for a group of displays and effects. Think of it
    like a layer in a design tool &mdash; each scene renders on top of the ones
    below, and effects only apply to their own scene.
  </p>
  <div class="placeholder">Screenshot: Scenes in the Layers panel</div>

  <h2>The Layers panel</h2>
  <p>Every scene has two sections:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Displays</strong> &mdash; visual elements rendered into the scene.</li>
    <li><strong>Effects</strong> &mdash; post-processing filters applied to the scene's output.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Adding a scene</h2>
  <p>
    Click the <strong>+</strong> in the Layers panel header. New scenes appear
    at the top of the stack.
  </p>

  <h2>Reordering</h2>
  <p>
    Drag scenes, displays, or effects to reorder them. The visual stack matches
    the panel order &mdash; the top entry in the Layers panel renders on top on
    the stage.
  </p>

  <h2>Enable / disable</h2>
  <p>
    Each scene, display, and effect has a toggle. Disabled items are skipped
    during render but stay in the project so you can bring them back.
  </p>

  <h2>Rename a layer</h2>
  <p>Double-click a layer name in the panel to rename it.</p>

  <h2>Blending scenes</h2>
  <p>
    Because effects are scoped per scene, you can apply a heavy blur or glitch
    to one scene without touching the others. Stack multiple scenes to build
    depth.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
